        <body>
            <div xml:id="d601">

                <pb facs="0665" n="561" sameAs="frus:frus1932v03/pg561"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">393.115 Radio Corporation of America/18:
                    Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Consul General at Shanghai (Cunningham) to the
                        Secretary of State</hi></head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Shanghai</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1932-03-11T11:00:00+08:00">March 11, 1932—11
                        a.m.</date><lb/>[Received March 11—2:40 a.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>179. Department’s telegram No. 69, March 3, 10 a.m., regarding radio station.
                    Withdrawal of Chinese forces has left radio station well within Japanese
                    military lines. I understand chief question now is repair of power lines and
                    connections with radio central office in Settlement. On March 5th and March 7th
                    at request of Shecklen I made representations to Japanese Consul General asking
                    that facilities be accorded to enable these repairs to be made as rapidly as
                    possible. <pb facs="0666" n="562" xml:id="pg562"/> Shecklen informs me that
                    Japanese are granting such facilities for repair work as could reasonably be
                    expected under the circumstances. He is keeping Radio Corporation fully
                    informed.</p>
                <p>Not repeated to the Legation. Copy to the Minister.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Cunningham</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
